From: kevinzhow <kevinzhow@local>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 19:40:00 +0900
Subject: Rfkill: without /dev/rfkill, Bluetooth is unblocked, not unknown

Without an rfkill interface nothing can block Bluetooth, yet the state stayed Unknown, which
bluedevil reads as "no Bluetooth hardware": its settings page said "No Bluetooth adapters found"
and hid the switch while BlueZ published a powered adapter. That is the case of a controller owned
by another system behind a BlueZ D-Bus service (the Android host, docs/73; the container has no
/dev/rfkill, and Android's bt_power rfkill does not follow its Bluetooth switch). When /dev/rfkill
does not exist the state is Unblocked and block/unblock do nothing; clients power the adapters
themselves (bluedevil does both). Other open failures keep the old behaviour.

Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-09-27
X-Rungic-Status: Inappropriate [platform choice: a system without rfkill; upstream assumes a kernel-managed controller]
X-Rungic-Tests: L3:bluetooth settings page (manual)
X-Rungic-Docs: docs/73
---
 src/rfkill.cpp | 14 ++++++++++++++
 src/rfkill_p.h |  1 +
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/rfkill.cpp b/src/rfkill.cpp
index 2a050fe..1b10b35 100644
--- a/src/rfkill.cpp
+++ b/src/rfkill.cpp
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include "debug.h"
 
 #ifdef Q_OS_LINUX
+#include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <stdint.h>
 #include <string.h>
@@ -172,6 +173,16 @@ void Rfkill::init()
 #ifdef Q_OS_LINUX
     d->m_readFd = ::open("/dev/rfkill", O_RDONLY | O_CLOEXEC);
 
+    if (d->m_readFd == -1 && errno == ENOENT) {
+        // No rfkill interface at all: no kill switch can block Bluetooth. Reporting Unknown instead
+        // reads as "no Bluetooth hardware" to clients (bluedevil), although adapters may be there,
+        // e.g. a controller that another system owns behind a BlueZ D-Bus service.
+        qCDebug(BLUEZQT) << "No /dev/rfkill: Bluetooth is not blocked by any switch";
+        d->m_noDevice = true;
+        d->m_state = Unblocked;
+        return;
+    }
+
     if (d->m_readFd == -1) {
         qCWarning(BLUEZQT) << "Cannot open /dev/rfkill for reading!";
         return;
@@ -258,6 +269,9 @@ void Rfkill::setSoftBlock(quint8 soft)
 #ifndef Q_OS_LINUX
     Q_UNUSED(soft)
 #else
+    if (d->m_noDevice) {
+        return; // nothing to switch; clients power the adapters themselves
+    }
     if (!d->m_thread) {
         d->m_thread = new RfkillThread(this);
     }
diff --git a/src/rfkill_p.h b/src/rfkill_p.h
index 861ddef..fb97c46 100644
--- a/src/rfkill_p.h
+++ b/src/rfkill_p.h
@@ -47,6 +47,7 @@ struct RfkillPrivate {
     Rfkill::State m_state = Rfkill::State::Unknown;
     QHash<quint32, Rfkill::State> m_devices;
     RfkillThread *m_thread = nullptr;
+    bool m_noDevice = false; // no /dev/rfkill: nothing blocks Bluetooth
 };
 
 } // namespace BluezQt
